34,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"Transport costs vary wildly depending on distance, and whole-building lets carry a risk where the group must cover the cost of any late cancellations.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"Cooking for ourselves on campus is a significant burden on the organizers, and the public booking process is an administrative nightmare involving a complex lottery.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Minako,,"The seniors believe that mixing year-groups only happens during shared evenings, and attendance records show that away camps often see a significant drop in participation.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"Inland facilities can be overcrowded with strict rules, and our own hall's vacation schedule is already quite crowded, which would force us into awkward training hours.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"Public beds are quite affordable, but a third of our members can't reach campus by nine in the morning without paying for overnight accommodation.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Asuka, if a third of the members need beds on campus, that adds a hidden cost to the ""local"" option. We must account for every single yen to avoid last year's deficit.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Ryuji, I agree. If we stay local, the administrative burden of arranging separate accommodation for those members, combined with the cooking duty, makes the campus option very unattractive.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Minako,,"If we want to improve bonding, we need everyone in the same place. If the campus option splits us up or the away camps discourage attendance, we need a careful balance.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Minako, the inland facilities might provide that shared space, but the reports of eight people to a room and strict curfews suggest the quality of rest would be poor.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Jonathan, if the inland facilities are that restrictive, the low cost of public beds might not be worth the trade-off in member well-being and training quality.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,"@Toshio, if the public booking process is as tedious as you say, the Mountain Training Centre might be a logistical trap regardless of the price.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Toshio,,Exactly. The risk of a single date shift forcing a total resubmission of a nine-page application is a burden we cannot afford to place on the organizers again.,"{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Minako,,"If we avoid the Mountain Training Centre and the Campus Intensive, the Coastal Lodge seems like the only remaining option that could keep us together in one place.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"The Coastal Lodge would provide the privacy and quiet we need for recovery, provided the owner's meal arrangements are reliable and the environment is conducive to sleep.","{""words"":27,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Ryuji, we must consider the risk you mentioned regarding whole-building lets. If the Coastal Lodge requires a fixed headcount, a few late cancellations could make the trip unfairly expensive for everyone else.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,"That is a valid concern, but it is a risk we can manage with a strict payment deadline. It is better than the financial uncertainty of transport or the administrative chaos of public bookings.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Toshio,,I agree. Removing the cooking duty and the lottery stress is a priority for the organizers. The Coastal Lodge seems the most sustainable choice for those of us running it.,"{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Minako,,"If the Coastal Lodge allows us to stay together in one building, it solves the bonding issue while avoiding the attendance drop-off we see with more restrictive public facilities.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree. Given the overcrowding at inland centers and the scheduling conflicts on campus, the Coastal Lodge is the most promising option for ensuring both quality rest and a cohesive group experience.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"I agree. While the financial risk of a whole-building let exists, it is a manageable one compared to the logistical failures of the other options. I vote for the Coastal Lodge.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
